  "transcript": "# United Nations Industrial Development Organization\n\n## Why now: a changed context\n> The UK thinks the world and its relationship with UNIDO have changed since 2012, so rejoining could be useful now.\n\nChair: Welcome. Our first big question: why should the UK rejoin UNIDO — the UN agency for industrial development — after leaving in 2012?\nMatt Toombs: The short answer is the context's different. Back in 2012 we had only a thin relationship. Since then we've worked with UNIDO on big programmes and built a deeper partnership.\nMatt Toombs: Industrial development — especially 'green' or low-carbon industrial growth — matters for developing countries' jobs and economies, and it matters for global climate goals.\nMatt Toombs: If the UK wants to help shape that process, win contracts for UK businesses and mobilise finance, being inside UNIDO gives us more influence than being outside.\n\n\n## Examples of existing UK–UNIDO work\n> The UK already works closely with UNIDO on several projects, giving practical reasons to rejoin.\n\nLord Boateng: Can you give concrete examples where our engagement has mattered?\nMatt Toombs: Yes. We worked with UNIDO through COP26 — that's the UN climate talks — and on green public procurement with India to boost demand for clean steel and concrete.\nMatt Toombs: There's an Accelerate-to-Demonstrate programme around £125 million. Also, UK work in Brazil — about £15 million of UK investment — has unlocked larger international and private finance.\nMatt Toombs: Those examples show small UK sums can be used to leverage much larger funding and real jobs on the ground.\n\n\n## Have real reforms happened at UNIDO?\n> UNIDO says it has become smaller, more transparent and has independent evaluation — the UK believes these are genuine improvements.\n\nBaroness Anelay of St Johns: When we left in 2012 there were governance problems. What reforms have actually happened?\nMatt Toombs: UNIDO has changed: it's leaner, publishes more about programme effectiveness, and has an independent evaluation unit. That's a clearer accountability picture than before.\nBaroness Anelay of St Johns: Other countries left too. Have you talked to them about coming back?\nMatt Toombs: We talk with like-minded countries but can't speak for them. Many industrialised countries — Japan, Italy, Germany and the EU — stayed involved, so the UK wouldn't be unusual in rejoining.\n\n\n## Climate finance, the poorest, and private money\n> UK membership could help scale projects and link public money to private investment, but access to climate finance for the poorest remains a big problem.\n\nLord Boateng: We spend less on overseas development now. How will this subscription to UNIDO help the poorest — for example on water and sanitation — when climate money often doesn't reach them?\nMatt Toombs: Our international climate finance — ICF, the money we spend on climate overseas — is smaller, so we must make it go further and mobilise other funds. Being inside UNIDO helps us scale up programmes rather than fund tiny one-offs.\nMatt Toombs: For instance, the Brazil example showed how UK money can unlock £250 million of other public funds and attract about £2 billion of private investment — that creates jobs and local benefits.\nBaroness Verma: Getting climate finance is very hard for on-the-ground projects. Is it time to change how funds are accessed and whether ODA still fits today's needs?\nMatt Toombs: Yes, access is a real challenge. We work with big funds like the Green Climate Fund to speed up access. ODA — official development assistance, public development money — must now focus on mobilising private finance and building scale.\nBaroness Verma: Are there rules stopping ODA working directly with private companies?\nMatt Toombs: There are constraints, but we actively use ODA to de‑risk projects and attract private capital. UK businesses are already working with UNIDO in Kenya on green tea, and on green steel in South Africa.\n\n\n## Safeguards: preventing backsliding and review plans\n> The UK sees membership as a way to influence UNIDO and plans a UK-led review of the decision in 2029 to check progress.\n\nLord Hannay of Chiswick: Organisations can slide back into bad habits. How will we stop UNIDO slipping, and will turf battles with other UN bodies be a problem?\nMatt Toombs: Being a member gives us more levers to shape UNIDO and spot problems early. Many UN agencies are also undergoing broader UN reforms that should make them leaner and reduce wasteful overlap.\nLord Hannay of Chiswick: Will there be a fixed review to check whether rejoining was the right move?\nMatt Toombs: Yes. The UK plans to review in 2029 whether rejoining has delivered the benefits we've set out. That review will be a UK assessment of our membership, not a UNIDO self-review.\nLord Hannay of Chiswick: Would you come back to this committee then?\nMatt Toombs: We'd expect to engage with Parliament and oversight as part of that review process.\nBaroness Lawlor: Quick one: what about big partners like the US, Australia and New Zealand — are they likely to rejoin?\nMatt Toombs: I won't speak for other governments. We work closely with Australia and New Zealand, and with the US where we share policy aims, but each country sets its own position.\nChair: Thanks — we're short on time so we'll end this public session and move to private questions.\n",
